        "text": "G.1 RKHS STRUCTURE\nFor a weak learner \u03bd, we define a covariance operator:\n\u03a3\u03bd [f ] = 1\nN k\u03bd(\u00b7,xN )f(xN ), \u03a3\u03bd : H \u2192 H . Using this operator, we define \u03a3f = \u222b V \u03a3\u03bdp(d\u03bd|f, \u03b2) and \u03a3 = \u222b V \u03a3\u03bd\u03c0(d\u03bd). These operators are typically referred to as covariance operators.\nHere we formulate and prove several lemmas about the RKHS structure and operators \u03a3,\u03a3f ,\u03a3\u03bd .\nLemma G.1. The following formula holds \u2200h, g \u2208 H:\n\u3008\u03a3[h], g\u3009H = 1\nN N\u2211 i=1 h(xi)g(xi).\nLemma G.2. (Covariation majorization) The following operator inequality holds \u2200\u03bd \u2208 V and \u2200f \u2208 H:\n\u03bbmax(\u03a3\u03bd) \u2264 1\n\u03bbmin(\u03a3f ) \u2265 1\nN\nProof. We will show that \u03bbmax(\u03a3) \u2264 1 where \u03c0 in the definition of \u03a3 can be chosen arbitrary. This way the statement would follow for \u03a3,\u03a3f ,\u03a3\u03bd by taking \u03c0 to be equal p(\u00b7 | f\u2217, \u03b2), p(\u00b7 | f, \u03b2), or \u03b4\u03bd(\u00b7) respectively. The operator \u03a3 is dual to KN = 1NK(xN ,xN ) since we can define the operator\nSN [f ] = [ \u3008f,K(\u00b7, x1)\u3009H, . . . , \u3008f,K(\u00b7, xN )\u3009H ] \u2208 RN ,\nso that \u03a3 = 1N S \u2217 NSN and KN = 1 N SNS \u2217 N and, thus, has the same spectra.\nTherefore, instead of \u03a3, we can study the maximal eigenvalue of KN but the latter admits another decomposition\nKN = \u222b V \u03a6T\u03bd { \u03a6\u03bd\u03a6 T \u03bd }\u2020 \u03a6\u03bd\u03c0(d\u03bd),\nwhere \u03a6\u03bd = \u03c6\u03bd(xN ) \u2208 RL\u03bd\u00d7N . The key observation is that the sub-expectation expression\n\u03a6T\u03bd { \u03a6\u03bd\u03a6 T \u03bd }\u2020 \u03a6\u03bd defines a matrix with maximal eigenvalue bounded by 1 since the former is an\northoprojector which is also shown in Ustimenko & Prokhorenkova (2021). Therefore, the expectation also inherits this bound on the maximal eigenvalue which with symmetry of KN gives us the desired inequality.\nNow we need to show that \u03bbmin(\u03a3f ) \u2265 1N . Similarly to the previous proof, we need to show that \u03bbmin(\u03a3f\u2217) \u2265 1N and then to replace \u03c0 \u2190 p(\u00b7|f, \u03b2). Consider the following formula:\n\u03a3 = 1\nN N\u2211 i=1 K(\u00b7, xi)\u2297H K(\u00b7, xi) ,\n\u03a3 = 1\nN N\u2211 i=1 K(xi, xi) ( K(\u00b7, xi)\u221a K(xi, xi) \u2297H K(\u00b7, xi)\u221a K(xi, xi) ) ,\nwhere (a \u2297H b)[c] = \u3008b, c\u3009Ha. If a = b and \u2016a\u2016H = 1, then 1 and 0 are the only eigenvalues of a \u2297H a. Then by induction assuming that we already summed up some of such rank one operators one of two scenarios can happen if we add one more rank one operator: either the rank does not increase or the rank increases by one. In the first case we obtain the operator that is greater in the operator sense then the previously obtained one whilst both of them share the same linear space as image and coimage which due to positive definiteness of both of them on the space can happen only if all eigenvalues of the new one greater or equal compared to the ones of the previous one. In the second scenario the newly added rank one operator adds new eigenvalue 1 due to symmetry of the operators. Finally, by noting that infx\u2208X K(x, x) \u2265 1, we obtain the bound.\nCorollary G.3. (Expected squared norm majorization by RKHS norm) The following bound holds \u2200f \u2208 H:\n\u2016f\u2016L2(\u03c1) \u2264 \u2016f\u2016H.\nProof. From \u03bbmax(\u03a3) \u2264 1 it follows that \u03bbmax(K) = limN\u2192\u221e \u03bbmax(\u03a3) \u2264 1. Let K\u22121 be taken as the unique inverse K\u22121 on F and for arbitrary f \u2208 L2(\u03c1) we consider L2(\u03c1) = F \u2295 F\u22a5 which implies f = f1 +f2, f1 \u2208 F , f2 \u2208 F\u22a5\u2200f \u2208 L2(\u03c1) and, thus, allows us to defineK\u22121f = K\u22121f1 \u2208\nF . Such definition of the inverse implies that \u03bbmin(K\u22121) = 1\u03bbmax(K) \u2265 1 which when substituted back into the product\n\u3008f, f\u3009H = \u3008K\u2212 1 2 f,K\u2212 12 f\u3009L2(\u03c1) = \u3008K \u22121f, f\u3009L2(\u03c1) implies the bound.\nLemma G.4. \u2016k\u03bd(xN ,xN )\u2016 \u2264 N for any \u03bd \u2208 V .\nProof. Consider S1, ..., SL\u03bd \u2014 unions such that Si = {xj |\u03c6 (i) \u03bd (xj) = 1}. Then, it is easy to see that\nk\u03bd = L\u03bd\u2211 i=1 w(i)\u03bd 1Ni\u03bd\u00d7Ni\u03bd ,\nwhere 1n\u00d7n is a matrix of size n\u00d7 n consisting of ones. Then, we note that \u20161n\u00d7n\u2016 = n and now the statement of the lemma follows.\nLemma G.5. For any \u03bd \u2208 V , we have k\u03bd(\u00b7,xN )[yN \u2212 f\u2217(xN )] = 0.\nProof. Follows from Lemma 3.7.\nLemma G.6 (Lemma 3.9 in the main text). Iterations f\u03c4 of Gradient Boosting (Algorithm 2) can be written in the form:\nf\u03c4+1 = ( 1\u2212 \u03bb\nN\n) f\u03c4 +\nN k\u03bd\u03c4 (\u00b7,xN )\n[ yN \u2212 f\u03c4 (xN ) ] = (1\u2212 \u03bb\nN )f\u03c4 + N k\u03bd\u03c4 (\u00b7,xN )\n[ f\u2217(xN )\u2212 f\u03c4 (xN ) ] ,\n\u03bd\u03c4 \u223c p(\u03bd|f\u03c4 , \u03b2) .\nProof. According to Algorithm 2:\nf\u03c4+1(\u00b7) = ( 1\u2212 \u03bb\nN\n) f\u03c4 (\u00b7) + \u2329 \u03c6\u03bd\u03c4 (\u00b7), \u03b8\u03c4 \u232a RL\u03bd\u03c4 for \u03b8\u03c4 = (\u2211N i=1 \u03c6 (j) \u03bd\u03c4 (xi)r (i) \u03c4\u2211N\ni=1 \u03c6 (j) \u03bd\u03c4 (xi)\n)L\u03bd\u03c4 j=1 .\nThus,\nf\u03c4+1 = ( 1\u2212 \u03bb\nN\n) f\u03c4 + 1\nN L\u03bd\u03c4\u2211 j=1 \u03c9j\u03bd\u03c4\u03c6 j \u03bd\u03c4 \u2211 i : \u03c6j\u03bd\u03c4 (xi)=1 ri\u03c4 .\nNow note that k\u03bd\u03c4 (\u00b7, xi) = \u03c9j\u03bd\u03c4\u03c6 j \u03bd\u03c4 (\u00b7), where j is such that \u03c6 j \u03bd\u03c4 (xi) = 1. From this the lemma follows.\nLemma G.7. Let A,B \u2208 B(H,H) be two PSD operators such that \u03beB \u2212 A and \u03beA\u2212 B are PSD for some \u03be \u2208 (1,\u221e). Let g, h \u2208 H two arbitrary vectors and \u03bb \u2208 R++ be a constant. Then:\n\u3008A[g] + \u03bb\u03beh,B[g] + \u03bbh\u3009H \u2265 1\n2\n( \u03be\u22121 \u2225\u2225A[g] + \u03bb\u03beh\u2225\u22252H \u2212 \u03be(\u03be2 \u2212 1)\u03bb2\u2016h\u20162H)\nProof. Consider the following equity:\n\u03be\u3008\u03be\u22121A[g] + \u03bbh,B[g] + \u03bbh\u3009H = \u03be\n2 (\u2225\u2225\u03be\u22121A[g] + \u03bbh\u2225\u22252H + \u2225\u2225B[g] + \u03bbh\u2225\u22252H \u2212 \u2225\u2225(B \u2212 \u03be\u22121A)[g]\u2225\u22252H) which is basically classical decomposition of the dot product \u3008x, y\u3009 = 12 ( \u2016x\u20162 + \u2016y\u20162\u2212\u2016x\u2212 y\u20162 ) .\nThen we note that ( 1\u2212\u03be\u22122 ) B\u2212 ( B\u2212\u03be\u22121A ) = \u03be\u22122(\u03beA\u2212B) is PSD by assumption which implies:\n\u3008A[g] + \u03bb\u03beh,B[g] + \u03bbh\u3009H \u2265 \u03be\n2 (\u2225\u2225\u03be\u22121A[g] + \u03bbh\u2225\u22252H + \u2225\u2225B[g] + \u03bbh\u2225\u22252H \u2212 (1\u2212 \u03be\u22122)2\u2225\u2225B[g]\u2225\u22252H) Finally, noting that \u03be 2\n2\u2212\u03be\u22122 \u2212 1 = \u03be2(1\u2212\u03be\u22122)2\n2\u2212\u03be\u22122 \u2264 \u03be 2 \u2212 1 the result directly follows from equity:\u2225\u2225B[g] + \u03bbh\u2225\u22252H \u2212 (1\u2212 \u03be\u22122)2\u2225\u2225B[g]\u2225\u22252H =\u2225\u2225\u03be\u22121\u221a2\u2212 \u03be\u22122B[g] + \u03bb \u03be\u22121 \u221a 2\u2212 \u03be\u22122 h \u2225\u22252 H \u2212 \u03bb 2 ( \u03be2 2\u2212 \u03be\u22122 \u2212 1 )\u2225\u2225h\u2225\u22252H\nDenote \u03ba(A,B) = \u2016IdH \u2212BA\u22121\u2016B(H,H) = \u2016(B \u2212A)A\u22121\u2016B(H,H). Lemma G.8. If \u03be\u22121K Kf \u03beK for \u03be > 1 then \u03ba(K,Kf ) \u2264 \u03be \u2212 1.\nProof. First we note that both operators are symmetric semi-positive definite in L2. Now let us look at Rayleigh quotient:\n\u2016(K \u2212Kf )K\u22121\u2016B(H,H) = max f\u2208F\\{0} \u2016(K \u2212Kf )K\u22121f\u2016H \u2016f\u2016H = max f\u2208F\\{0} \u2016K\u2212 12 (K \u2212Kf )K\u2212 1 2 f\u2016L2 \u2016f\u2